# The annotations of tape block 2, the BASIC program "R-TYPE" at PROG $5CCB-$700F (4933 bytes): the source of
# src/tape/basic.asm (../../kit/mkgame.py --part src/tape/basic.json). Format: ../../../../docs/ann-format.md.
# Witness: c1 (work/stage1/c1, a 48K from power-on, the tape at signal speed): the ROM's LD-BYTES wrote $5CCB-$700F;
# work/stage2/c1/chain (c1 frames 2081-2398) ran CopyChain.

@module basic ; The BASIC: one line whose USR enters the machine code after it, and the enciphered chain
@about The program is one line, number 0 (LIST and EDIT never reach a line 0): INK 0, PAPER 0, RANDOMIZE USR ((PEEK 23635+256*PEEK 23636)+10). The 10 is only the shown digits: the hidden five-byte number after them is 59, so the USR enters PROG+59, the first byte after the line's end mark: CopyChain at $5D06 (PROG = $5CCB in c1).
@about CopyChain copies the 4840 bytes from PROG+$5C ($5D27, ChainTape) to $EC00-$FEE7 and returns into them: the decryption chain (src/loader/chain.asm, its plain view). ChainTape is assembled from chain-tape.bin, which tools/build.sh makes from the plain view with tools/speedlock7.py tape.

$5CCB b BasicLine0 ; Line 0: INK 0 PAPER 0 RANDOMIZE USR ((PEEK 23635+256*PEEK 23636)+10), the 10 hiding 59
> The ROM's line format: the line number high byte first, the length (55) low byte first, the tokens, $0D. Each number in the line is its digits followed by $0E and the five bytes the interpreter really uses: the digits 23635, 256 and 23636 carry their own numbers, the digits 10 carry 59. Read by the ROM's interpreter in c1; no machine code reads it.
$5CCB BasicLineNumber ; the line number, high byte first: 0
$5CCD BasicLineLength ; the line's length after these four bytes, low byte first: 55
$5CCF ; INK 0, PAPER 0 (control codes $10 and $11 with their colour)
$5CD3 ; RANDOMIZE USR ( (
$5CD7 ; PEEK "23635" and its number 23635 (PROG's low byte)
$5CE3 ; + "256" and its number
$5CED ; * PEEK "23636" and its number (PROG's high byte)
$5CFA ; ) + "10" and the hidden number
$5CFF BasicHiddenNumber ; the five-byte number the interpreter adds: 59 (the shown digits say 10)
$5D04 ; ) and the end of line mark $0D

$5D06 c CopyChain ; Blacken the attributes, copy the chain to $EC00-$FEE7 and return into it
> Entered by line 0's USR (PROG+59). Interrupts off; fills $5800-$5BFF with zero: the 768 attribute bytes (INK 0 on PAPER 0: the screen goes black, the bitmap stays) and the printer buffer $5B00-$5BFF, border black. HL = PROG + $5C (ChainTape, found from the system variable PROG at 23635 rather than assumed); DE = $EC00, BC = 4840; $EC00 pushed, then LDIR and RET: the RET enters the chain's first layer at $EC00. SP stays where the ROM's USR left it.
> c1: the LDIR wrote $EC00-$FEE7 in work/stage2/c1/chain.
> Witness: L1 1 (stage2/c1/chain).
$5D16 ; HL = PROG + $5C: the chain's first byte in the program
$5D23 ; the RET below goes to $EC00

$5D27 b ChainTape ; The decryption chain as the tape holds it (4840 bytes, copied to $EC00)
> The bytes of $EC00-$FEE7 before any layer ran. src/loader/chain.asm is their plain view (each byte after the last layer that rewrites it); tools/speedlock7.py tape turns the plain view back into these bytes, and tools/build.sh checks the whole tape against the original. Read by CopyChain's LDIR in c1.
@res tape/chain-tape.bin

$700F b BasicEnd ; The block's last byte, past the copy: $54 no code reads
> CopyChain copies 4840 bytes, $5D27-$700E; the block's 4933 bytes end one byte later. Nothing reads it after the ROM's load (c1: CopyChain's LDIR stops at $700E): a byte of the BASIC block left over past the chain.
